      "id": 1,
      "text": "King William County Courthouse, built 1725, is the oldest courthouse in continuous use in the United States.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/King_William%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The courthouse is also the oldest public building in Virginia still in use, a testament to colonial architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kwc.gov/302/About-King-William"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "King William was named after King William III of England, who reigned 1689‑1702.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kwc.gov/302/About-King-William"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area was once part of the Powhatan Confederacy, led by Chief Wahunsonacock, before English settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
